1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:06,633
In 2019, the President of the United States
made an offer that sounded like a joke.

2
00:00:06,700 --> 00:00:08,967
He wanted to buy Greenland.

3
00:00:09,033 --> 00:00:11,133
The biggest island on Earth.

4
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:14,967
Denmark's prime minister
called the idea absurd.

5
00:00:15,033 --> 00:00:20,267
And Greenland's answer was even
shorter: we are not for sale.

6
00:00:20,333 --> 00:00:24,200
Then, in 2025, he brought it up again.

7
00:00:24,267 --> 00:00:27,333
And this time, nobody was laughing.

8
00:00:27,400 --> 00:00:29,700
But here's what most people miss.

9
00:00:29,767 --> 00:00:32,267
This isn't a crazy new idea.

10
00:00:32,333 --> 00:00:35,500
Buying land is how America was built.

11
00:00:35,567 --> 00:00:40,033
Close to forty percent of the
United States wasn't conquered.

12
00:00:40,100 --> 00:00:41,267
It was bought.

13
00:00:41,333 --> 00:00:46,467
With cash, with gold, and
sometimes with a deal so lopsided

14
00:00:46,533 --> 00:00:49,167
it still doesn't feel real.

15
00:00:49,233 --> 00:00:52,800
So why does America keep
trying to buy Greenland?

16
00:00:52,867 --> 00:00:57,833
To answer that, we need to look
at every deal that came before it.

17
00:00:57,900 --> 00:01:02,600
Because every single one of
them follows the same pattern.

18
00:01:02,833 --> 00:01:10,100
In 1803, the United States was a young
country squeezed along the Atlantic coast.

19
00:01:10,167 --> 00:01:13,400
Its western border was
the Mississippi River.

20
00:01:13,467 --> 00:01:19,767
And at the mouth of that river sat one city
that controlled everything: New Orleans.

21
00:01:19,833 --> 00:01:24,400
Farmers in Kentucky and Ohio shipped
their crops down the river and out

22
00:01:24,467 --> 00:01:26,400
through that one port.

23
00:01:26,467 --> 00:01:27,767
The problem?

24
00:01:27,833 --> 00:01:33,067
New Orleans, and the huge territory
behind it, now belonged to France.

25
00:01:33,133 --> 00:01:37,233
And France was ruled
by Napoleon Bonaparte.

26
00:01:37,300 --> 00:01:42,767
President Thomas Jefferson sent his
envoys to Paris with a simple mission:

27
00:01:42,833 --> 00:01:44,567
buy New Orleans.

28
00:01:44,633 --> 00:01:49,633
He authorized them to spend up to ten
million dollars, just for the city

29
00:01:49,700 --> 00:01:51,800
and a strip of coast.

30
00:01:51,867 --> 00:01:54,800
But Napoleon had a problem of his own.

31
00:01:54,867 --> 00:01:59,033
His army had just been crushed
in Haiti, where enslaved people

32
00:01:59,100 --> 00:02:03,467
had risen up and defeated one of
the strongest armies in Europe.

33
00:02:03,533 --> 00:02:08,567
Without Haiti and its sugar, his
American empire made no sense.

34
00:02:08,633 --> 00:02:11,100
And a new war with Britain was coming.

35
00:02:11,167 --> 00:02:12,400
He needed money.

36
00:02:12,467 --> 00:02:13,867
Fast.

37
00:02:13,933 --> 00:02:17,700
So the French made an offer
the Americans never expected.

38
00:02:17,767 --> 00:02:19,267
Not just the city.

39
00:02:19,333 --> 00:02:21,367
All of Louisiana.

40
00:02:21,433 --> 00:02:25,800
The price: fifteen million dollars, for
about eight hundred and twenty-eight

41
00:02:25,867 --> 00:02:27,767
thousand square miles.

42
00:02:27,833 --> 00:02:30,133
Roughly three cents an acre.

43
00:02:30,200 --> 00:02:33,900
Overnight, the United
States doubled in size.

44
00:02:33,967 --> 00:02:38,367
Today, that land is all
or part of fifteen states.

45
00:02:38,433 --> 00:02:39,833
And here's the twist.

46
00:02:39,900 --> 00:02:44,700
The deal was financed with bonds
sold through British and Dutch banks.

47
00:02:44,767 --> 00:02:49,633
So in a way, British money helped
pay Napoleon, right before he went

48
00:02:49,700 --> 00:02:52,667
back to war against Britain.

49
00:02:52,933 --> 00:02:55,800
The next deal came in 1819.

50
00:02:55,867 --> 00:02:59,600
Spain owned Florida, but
it could barely control it.

51
00:02:59,667 --> 00:03:01,933
So Spain handed it over.

52
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:03,533
The surprising part?

53
00:03:03,600 --> 00:03:06,200
Spain didn't get a single dollar.

54
00:03:06,267 --> 00:03:10,467
The United States simply agreed
to pay up to five million dollars

55
00:03:10,533 --> 00:03:14,667
in claims that American
citizens had against Spain.

56
00:03:14,733 --> 00:03:16,900
Then came the hardest case.

57
00:03:16,967 --> 00:03:21,533
In 1846, the United States
went to war with Mexico.

58
00:03:21,600 --> 00:03:27,567
When it ended two years later, Mexico gave
up more than half a million square miles:

59
00:03:27,633 --> 00:03:32,667
today's California, Nevada,
Utah, and most of Arizona.

60
00:03:32,733 --> 00:03:36,833
The United States paid fifteen
million dollars for it.

61
00:03:36,900 --> 00:03:41,167
Was it a purchase, or a price
tag stapled to a conquest?

62
00:03:41,233 --> 00:03:43,100
Historians still argue.

63
00:03:43,167 --> 00:03:45,900
But one detail stands out.

64
00:03:45,967 --> 00:03:52,000
Nine days before the treaty was signed, a
worker found gold in a California river.

65
00:03:52,067 --> 00:03:54,000
Neither government knew yet.

66
00:03:54,067 --> 00:03:59,033
Within a few years, a single year of
California gold was worth more than

67
00:03:59,100 --> 00:04:01,800
the entire price of the deal.

68
00:04:01,867 --> 00:04:06,800
Five years later, America came
back for one more strip of desert.

69
00:04:06,867 --> 00:04:10,967
Ten million dollars for
southern Arizona and New Mexico.

70
00:04:11,033 --> 00:04:11,700
Why?

71
00:04:11,767 --> 00:04:13,233
A railroad.

72
00:04:13,300 --> 00:04:18,833
Engineers wanted the flattest route to
the Pacific, and it ran through Mexico.

73
00:04:18,900 --> 00:04:24,100
But the strangest deal of all was
still to come, in the frozen north.

74
00:04:24,333 --> 00:04:27,067
Now jump to the far north.

75
00:04:27,133 --> 00:04:31,000
In the 1860s, Alaska belonged
to the Russian Empire.

76
00:04:31,067 --> 00:04:33,133
But Russia had a problem.

77
00:04:33,200 --> 00:04:37,500
It had just lost the Crimean
War against Britain and France.

78
00:04:37,567 --> 00:04:43,400
Its treasury was strained, and Alaska
was almost impossible to defend.

79
00:04:43,467 --> 00:04:46,433
Right next door sat British Canada.

80
00:04:46,500 --> 00:04:51,167
If another war broke out,
Britain could simply take Alaska.

81
00:04:51,233 --> 00:04:56,733
So the Tsar made a cold calculation:
better to sell it to America than lose

82
00:04:56,800 --> 00:04:59,067
it to Britain for nothing.

83
00:04:59,133 --> 00:05:04,633
In 1867, Secretary of State
William Seward signed the deal.

84
00:05:04,700 --> 00:05:07,600
Seven point two million dollars.

85
00:05:07,667 --> 00:05:13,300
About two cents an acre, for a territory
more than twice the size of Texas.

86
00:05:13,367 --> 00:05:17,433
The canceled check is still
kept at the National Archives.

87
00:05:17,500 --> 00:05:18,833
Critics mocked it.

88
00:05:18,900 --> 00:05:21,300
They called it Seward's Folly.

89
00:05:21,367 --> 00:05:22,967
Seward's Icebox.

90
00:05:23,033 --> 00:05:26,300
Why pay for a frozen wasteland?

91
00:05:26,367 --> 00:05:28,000
Then came gold.

92
00:05:28,067 --> 00:05:32,900
Then, in 1968, oil at Prudhoe
Bay, the largest oil field

93
00:05:32,967 --> 00:05:35,033
ever found in North America.

94
00:05:35,100 --> 00:05:40,500
And during the Cold War, Alaska became
America's front line, just a few miles

95
00:05:40,567 --> 00:05:44,100
from Soviet territory
across the Bering Strait.

96
00:05:44,167 --> 00:05:47,133
Nobody calls it folly anymore.

97
00:05:47,367 --> 00:05:54,133
In 1917, during World War One, America
bought land from Denmark for the first time.

98
00:05:54,200 --> 00:05:58,433
Washington feared Germany could
grab the Danish West Indies,

99
00:05:58,500 --> 00:06:04,167
islands sitting right on the shipping
lanes to the brand new Panama Canal.

100
00:06:04,233 --> 00:06:08,000
The price: twenty-five
million dollars in gold.

101
00:06:08,067 --> 00:06:11,933
Today, we call them the US Virgin Islands.

102
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:16,900
And it was not the last time
America knocked on Denmark's door.

103
00:06:17,133 --> 00:06:22,600
In 1946, right after World War
Two, the Truman administration made

104
00:06:22,667 --> 00:06:29,300
Denmark a secret offer: one hundred
million dollars, in gold, for Greenland.

105
00:06:29,367 --> 00:06:31,400
Denmark said no.

106
00:06:31,467 --> 00:06:35,600
And the offer stayed hidden
for decades, until declassified

107
00:06:35,667 --> 00:06:39,433
documents surfaced in 1991.

108
00:06:39,500 --> 00:06:42,933
But America got most of
what it wanted anyway.

109
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:48,267
Under a defense agreement with Denmark,
it built a giant air base in the far

110
00:06:48,333 --> 00:06:49,900
north of Greenland.

111
00:06:49,967 --> 00:06:54,733
Today it's called Pituffik Space
Base, and it watches the skies

112
00:06:54,800 --> 00:06:58,233
for missiles coming over the North Pole.

113
00:06:58,500 --> 00:07:01,467
So why does Greenland matter so much?

114
00:07:01,533 --> 00:07:03,200
Look at the top of the map.

115
00:07:03,267 --> 00:07:08,900
The shortest path between Russia and
the United States runs over the Arctic,

116
00:07:08,967 --> 00:07:10,933
right past Greenland.

117
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:13,000
And the Arctic is changing.

118
00:07:13,067 --> 00:07:17,600
As the ice melts, new shipping
routes are opening up, and the ground

119
00:07:17,667 --> 00:07:20,633
underneath is becoming easier to reach.

120
00:07:20,700 --> 00:07:25,833
Ships sailing between Asia and Europe
could one day cut thousands of miles

121
00:07:25,900 --> 00:07:28,567
by going over the top of the world.

122
00:07:28,633 --> 00:07:33,033
Under that ground are some of the
largest deposits of rare earth

123
00:07:33,100 --> 00:07:34,900
elements in the world.

124
00:07:34,967 --> 00:07:40,900
The metals inside your phone, your
electric car, and a fighter jet.

125
00:07:40,967 --> 00:07:45,833
Right now, China processes
around ninety percent of them.

126
00:07:45,900 --> 00:07:49,300
Greenland could be one
way to break that grip.

127
00:07:49,367 --> 00:07:52,933
And America is not the only one looking.

128
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:59,667
In 2018, a Chinese state-owned company
bid to build airports in Greenland.

129
00:07:59,733 --> 00:08:03,833
Denmark stepped in and
paid for them itself.

130
00:08:03,900 --> 00:08:08,867
Greenland itself has only about
fifty-six thousand people.

131
00:08:08,933 --> 00:08:15,100
And Denmark covers a big part
of its budget every single year.

132
00:08:15,333 --> 00:08:17,567
So look at every deal again.

133
00:08:17,633 --> 00:08:20,300
Napoleon needed cash for a war.

134
00:08:20,367 --> 00:08:22,600
Spain couldn't hold Florida.

135
00:08:22,667 --> 00:08:25,167
Mexico had just lost a war.

136
00:08:25,233 --> 00:08:27,233
Russia was afraid of Britain.

137
00:08:27,300 --> 00:08:29,800
Denmark was afraid of Germany.

138
00:08:29,867 --> 00:08:34,267
Every time, America bought
land from someone in trouble.

139
00:08:34,333 --> 00:08:36,633
Greenland breaks the pattern.

140
00:08:36,700 --> 00:08:38,700
Denmark isn't desperate.

141
00:08:38,767 --> 00:08:43,367
And this time, the people who
actually live there have a voice.

142
00:08:43,433 --> 00:08:48,933
Greenland runs most of its own affairs,
and by law it has the right to choose

143
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:51,100
independence.

144
00:08:51,333 --> 00:08:54,867
So the real question isn't
how much Greenland costs.

145
00:08:54,933 --> 00:08:57,800
It's who Greenlanders want to belong to.

146
00:08:57,867 --> 00:09:02,000
And that is one deal
money alone can't close.

147
00:09:02,067 --> 00:09:06,000
If you want to see what's really
behind the map, subscribe.

148
00:09:06,067 --> 00:09:08,233
See you in the next one.
